From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jose Martins <josemartins90@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 20 May 2026 16:45:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 06/13] drivers: riscv_aia: always configure sourcecfg on irq
 priority set

An APLIC source with SM=0 (inactive) is never forwarded regardless of
its enable bit. Guard against this by always calling
riscv_aia_config_source(), defaulting to level-high when the caller
passes flags=0 (the standard 'no special type' sentinel).

Signed-off-by: Jose Martins <josemartins90@gmail.com>
(cherry picked from commit df3dae8eb6fd1804fcdbe459536b7f75cb1d4be8)
---
 soc/common/riscv-privileged/soc_common_irq.c | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/soc/common/riscv-privileged/soc_common_irq.c b/soc/common/riscv-privileged/soc_common_irq.c
index 55e09fe866f..5d943b0df50 100644
--- a/soc/common/riscv-privileged/soc_common_irq.c
+++ b/soc/common/riscv-privileged/soc_common_irq.c
@@ -196,9 +196,10 @@ void z_riscv_irq_priority_set(unsigned int irq, unsigned int prio, uint32_t flag
 		return;
 	}
 
-	if (flags != 0) {
-		riscv_aia_config_source(irq, flags);
-	}
+	/* Always configure sourcecfg; SM=0 (inactive) means the APLIC never
+	 * forwards the source regardless of the enable bit. Default to
+	 * level-high when flags=0 (the standard "no special type" value). */
+	riscv_aia_config_source(irq, flags != 0 ? flags : APLIC_SM_LEVEL_HIGH);
 
 	/* Set priority if direct delivery mode is enabled.
 	 * AIA-IMSIC priority is handled via IMSIC EITHRESHOLD or EIID ordering.
